Organization = {COMMISS EUROPEAN COMMUNITIES; US DOE; US ENVIRONM PROTECT AGCY; ENNTE
   NUOVE TECNOL ENERGIA \& AMBIENTE; IST SUPER SANITA; INTER CTR THEORET
   PHYS; INTER CTR THEORET \& APPL ECOL},
Abstract = {The World Health Organization (WHO), Regional Office for Europe,
   organised a meeting of a working group on indoor air quality in Eilat,
   Israel, from 28 March to 4 April 1993. The aim was to develop a
   risk-based approach to health criteria for radon indoors. The Group
   reviewed the latest epidemiological data from occupational and
   non-occupational radon exposure, animal experiments and dosimetry. The
   Group issued 14 conclusions and 23 recommendations on radon related risk
   to health, on risk management and risk communication. In summary, radon
   was confirmed as a human carcinogen. Indoor radon exposures resulting in
   individual risks exceeding 10(-3) per year are to be considered as
   severe and risk reduction programmes implemented. Guidance on risk
   management and communication is offered to national authorities.},
