Journal = {INDIAN JOURNAL OF OTOLARYNGOLOGY AND HEAD \& NECK SURGERY},
Year = {2025},
Month = {2025 JUN 20},
Abstract = {Giant osteomas of the ethmoid sinus are exceptionally rare, with only a
   handful of cases documented in medical literature, there exists
   controversy surrounding the clinical representation and their optimal
   treatment approaches. Some cases show no symptoms, while others mimic
   sinus infections. Complications like chronic sinusitis and orbital
   cellulitis are common, highlighting the importance of understanding
   growth patterns and potential issues with these benign tumors. we
   present the case of a 52-year-old patient who had a huge ethmoidal
   osteoma extending into the orbit, revealed by exophthalmos and
   oculomotor paralysis, the tumor was successfully removed using an
   external approach combined with intranasal endoscopy, in this article we
   discuss the different surgical steps of this technique, as well as a
   review of the literature on this pathology, the place of the external
   approach, its pros and cons in comparison with other techniques.},
